// @expect-error Function 'narrowed' returns 'ushort', but this value has type 'char'. A 'char' is a character's number in Unicode, its code point, which can be as large as 1114111, and the largest value of 'ushort' is 65535. Write 'letter as ushort' if the character's code point is at most 65535.
// @expect-error Variable 'asByte' has type 'byte', but its value has type 'char'. A 'char' is a character's number in Unicode, its code point, which can be as large as 1114111, and the largest value of 'byte' is 255. Write 'letter as byte' if the character's code point is at most 255.
// @expect-error Variable 'asShort' has type 'short', but its value has type 'char'. A 'char' is a character's number in Unicode, its code point, which can be as large as 1114111, and the largest value of 'short' is 32767. Write 'letter as short' if the character's code point is at most 32767.
// @expect-error Argument 1 of 'takesShort' has type 'char', but parameter 'value' has type 'short'. A 'char' is a character's number in Unicode, its code point, which can be as large as 1114111, and the largest value of 'short' is 32767. Write 'letter as short' if the character's code point is at most 32767.
// @expect-error 'glyph.code' has type 'byte', but the value assigned to it has type 'char'. A 'char' is a character's number in Unicode, its code point, which can be as large as 1114111, and the largest value of 'byte' is 255. Write 'letter as byte' if the character's code point is at most 255.
// @expect-error Element 1 has type 'char', but the elements of this array have type 'byte'. A 'char' is a character's number in Unicode, its code point, which can be as large as 1114111, and the largest value of 'byte' is 255. Write 'letter as byte' if the character's code point is at most 255.
// @expect-error build failed with 6 problems
// A 'char' value does not convert on its own to an integer type too narrow for every code point,
// so 'byte', 'short' and 'ushort' refuse it with the largest value each has and the cast that
// converts a character known to fit.
class Glyph {
    byte code = 0;
}

takesShort(short value) {
}

narrowed(char letter) -> ushort {
    return letter;
}

main() -> int {
    char letter = 'a';
    byte asByte = letter;
    short asShort = letter;
    takesShort(letter);
    let glyph = new Glyph();
    glyph.code = letter;
    byte[] codes = [letter];
    return 0;
}
